# Formatted with ruff's default settings: uvx ruff format <this file>
# The shebang's "# python" is a comment env -S drops; editors read it to pick Python.
# Tests: pantheon test unit runs the unit tests at the end of this file.

"""Pantheon: agent collaboration and convergence.

Each agent is a real CLI (claude, codex, or agy). A mode names its agents, and a plan: stages in
which the agents take turns, and in a loop, take them again until one of them finds the stage's
halting prompt true. A stage can also let its agents stop the plan, when they find it should not go
on, or let one agent pick the track the plan goes on with. A multi agent has an instance for each
tool: each on a track of its own, or in a stage where they work together, all taking each turn at
once (which then halts a loop only if all of them halt it); pantheon start help MODE shows a mode's
agents and plan, and pantheon test SCENARIO MODE tries it on a toy task. A mode can also start an
agent again, fresh, for each of its turns, as bakeoff and develop do their reviewers, so no review
leans on the last one: each start is a new generation of that agent, which its tab shows, as in
"reviewer-codex (2)", and the room notes. An agent that exits starts again the same way at its next
turn. Every agent knows the team, the plan, and the room, and acts when it sees a reason; once the
plan is done, the agents keep running, and you give them more work in the room, unless the session
stops them then (start --stop-when-done).
Pantheon shows the team in a chat-room feed (the room), gives each agent a tab with a live copy of
its screen, and lets you "transport" into an agent's own terminal when it needs you. Ctrl-] or the
button on tmux's status line brings you back. The top row has a tab for each session you have open
({ and } switch them); the row below has its agents' tabs. pantheon help lists the commands.

Agents keep your own setup: agent files, skills, MCP servers, and permission rules. An ollama
agent is claude with the ollama server as its API; if claude keeps an API key from a Console login,
it sends that key to the server too, so a server on another machine needs the ollama_remote
setting. Pantheon types each turn's instructions straight into its agent. The last message of the
agent's turn is its report, which ends with a "PANTHEON[status]: <status>" line; when the work is a
file or a branch, the message says where. The room is the team's channel: an agent reads it in a
file, and posts to it with a "PANTHEON[say]: <message>" line at the end of its turn's last
message. Pantheon gives each post to every other agent, which decides
whether it needs to act. So an agent can ask the team a question, or wake another agent. You can
post too (ctrl+s): to answer, or to give a team that is done more work. Only those posts reach the
agents as the user's. Pantheon's own lines in the room go to no agent. Each session has a private
tmux server, so agents keep running when pantheon exits. Each tool reports through hooks. Claude's
and codex's bring each turn's last message; agy (Antigravity CLI) reads its hooks from the run
dir, and its last message is in the transcript its hooks name; pantheon sees its dialogs on its
screen. The room names the model each agent really runs, and claude's effort, where they differ
from what pantheon asked for. Unless it runs from a clone of gregschmit/dotfiles (git updates
that), pantheon looks on GitHub for a newer version at most every 12 hours.

Files: ~/.local/state/pantheon (or $PANTHEON_HOME) holds config.toml and one sqlite file per
session, sessions/<slug>.sqlite3. That file is the whole session: its record, its lock, the prompts,
and the reports. Agents read plain files, so while they run, pantheon keeps the room's newest
entries in run/<slug>/room.txt, and each report in a file beside it.
"""

